\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/instrument-types}

An instrument type is a classification of \emph{what an instrument is}:
stock, bond, cryptocurrency, commodity, currency, index, fund.  This is a
different ontology from data source kinds, which classify \emph{how we talk
to a source}.

Names are canonicalized by \texttt{canonicalize-slug}: trimmed and
down-cased, so \texttt{"Cryptocurrency"} and \texttt{" cryptocurrency "}
are the same type.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{instrument-type}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \texttt{name}, and
\texttt{description}.  The accessors are \lispId{instrument-type-id},
\lispId{instrument-type-name}, and \lispId{instrument-type-description}.
\texttt{instrument-type-p} is true of instances.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{standard-instrument-types}

The conventional \texttt{(name description)} pairs.  A fresh copy is
returned, so callers may mutate the result freely.

The seeded names are \texttt{stock}, \texttt{bond}, \texttt{commodity},
\texttt{currency}, \texttt{cryptocurrency}, \texttt{index}, and
\texttt{fund}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-instrument-type}

Find or create the instrument type with the given name and return it.
\texttt{:description}, when given, fills in a missing description on an
existing type.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(make-instrument-type \lispVar{name} \&key \lispVar{description})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{instrument-type-by-name}

The instrument type whose canonical name is \lispVar{name}, or
\texttt{nil}.  This does not create a row.

\lispFunctionSubsection{instrument-type-id-for}

The database \textsc{Uuid} of \lispVar{type}, or \texttt{nil} when
\lispVar{type} is \texttt{nil}.  \lispVar{type} may be an
\texttt{instrument-type} object or a name string (which is found or
created).

\lispFunctionSubsection{all-instrument-types}

Every instrument type, alphabetically by name.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ensure-standard-instrument-types}

Insert the standard types if they are not already present, and return
them.  Call this once after the migrations have been applied.
